          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Why do some categories need a fourth or fifth character while others do not?",
          "answer": "Most ICD-10-CM categories need extra characters because the extra characters add detail. The retrieved guidance shows how this works in practice, but it does not give a single general reason for the rule.\n\n- **Hypertension (I10) needs none.** The coding guidance says I10 is \"a complete code at three characters.\" Most categories need a fourth, fifth, or sixth character before the code can be used. Asking for \"more specificity\" on I10 cannot be satisfied, because the classification has no finer version. (Source: *Coding hypertension: I10 and the hypertensive disease family*.)\n- **COPD (J44) uses the fourth character for complications.** Its fourth character records whether an acute lower respiratory infection or an acute exacerbation is present at the encounter, or whether neither is. (Source: *Coding chronic obstructive pulmonary disease: J44 and its two complication codes*.)\n- **Type 2 diabetes (E11) is split by cause.** The chapter groups diabetes by cause rather than by treatment, and insulin use does not change the category. (Source: *Coding type 2 diabetes: E11 and its complication subcategories*.)\n- **Injuries use further characters for the stage of care.** The structure guidance says injury codes distinguish initial treatment, subsequent treatment, and sequela. It also says a category that needs a seventh character needs it even when the characters in between are empty, and the placeholder X holds those positions. (Source: *ICD-10-CM code structure and the conventions that govern selection*.)\n\nThe retrieved passages do not explain why some categories were designed with no subdivisions and others with several. They also do not give a full list of which categories need which number of characters. For that, the official ICD-10-CM tabular list is the authoritative reference, and a trained medical coder can confirm the rules for a specific code.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
